description:
Goal: Deliver a small set of well-documented helper shims and examples that make Redwood's TypeScript SDK streaming APIs ergonomic across Node (native Web Streams/legacy Readable), Deno, and modern browsers. This ticket focuses on: (1) clear API overloads that accept Response, ReadableStream, AsyncIterable<string|Uint8Array|TokenChunk>, or Node Readable; (2) reliable cancellation propagation to server-side AbortSignals; (3) backpressure semantics and memory boundedness; (4) concise code examples for Node (fetch/undici), Deno runtime, and browsers (fetch streaming + transform streams); (5) packaging guidance (ESM-only entrypoints, optional polyfill hints).

We will NOT implement a large polyfill bundle — instead we provide tiny shims + docs and explicit guidance for applications to opt-in to polyfills when targeting older runtimes. The surfaced helpers aim to be tree-shakeable and documented with migration examples.

Example consumer ergonomics we want to support (non-normative):

1) const stream = client.chat.stream({ ... });
   for await (const token of toAsyncIterable(stream)) { ... }

2) const ws = await client.chat.streamAsWebStream({ ... });
   const reader = ws.getReader(); // browser/Node native WebStream

3) // Accept a Response directly (fetch)
   await client.chat.consumeFetchResponse(fetchResp, { onToken: (t) => ... });

Code snippets below will be referenced in the docs and examples folder.
